        "message": "Here are three practical ways to handle this, depending on how you want to approach it: 1. **Be blunt about the pattern:** Have one firm, direct conversation (or send a clear text) pointing out that this is the third time. Keep it brief: *\"I’ve brought this up twice, and my things are still being used. Going forward, my kitchen items are completely off-limits.\"* Putting it in text also creates a clear record.\n2. **Remove the access:** If verbal boundaries aren't sticking, physically separate your items. Move your cookware, staples, or dishes into a labeled bin, a designated locked/separate cabinet, or your room. It’s inconvenient, but it removes the temptation and the friction immediately.\n3. **Set clear consequences:** Let them know what happens if it continues—whether that means replacing anything they consume or damage immediately, splitting shared kitchen costs differently, or involving your landlord/lease agreement if personal property issues persist. You can pick the approach that fits best—either drawing a hard line conversationally, physically removing access, or both.",
